<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Peru</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Charcas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> was the name of the highland territory of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of Charcas in the Spanish empire, corresponding roughly to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> until 1776 and then to the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. It declared independence in 1825 as the Republic of Bolivia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-peru.jpg" alt="Upper Peru" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: previous version Ignaciogavira; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Alto Perú in Spanish, was the land governed by the Real Audiencia of Charcas. It comprised the governorships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a>, Cochabamba, Chiquitos, Moxos and Charcas, the last since renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a>. The name itself arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> towards the end of the 18th century, after the audiencia had been moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> to the new Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in 1776.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the wars of independence the region was contested. The Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> treated Charcas as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> and elected representatives of the provinces of Upper Peru to the Congress of the United Provinces of the Río de la Plata, while the viceroy of Peru, Abascal, incorporated Upper Peru, Charcas included, into his own viceroyalty. Royalist forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-antonio-olaneta">Pedro Antonio Olañeta</a> were the last to resist there after the defeat of the Spanish royalists at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> in December 1824.</p><p>In the power vacuum that followed, the governor of Chiquitos, Colonel Sebastián Ramos, fearing punishment by the new revolutionary government, sent Captain José María de Velasco to the Brazilian province of Mato Grosso in March 1825. A treaty annexing Chiquitos to Mato Grosso was passed there in April by only three of the seven council members and without the knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>. It was annulled in May, and an Indigenous uprising in the village of San Miguel forced the Brazilian troops to withdraw, reportedly looting as they went. Pedro I condemned the annexation in August.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> took the incursion for a local initiative and sought a peaceful settlement, while warning that any attempt to keep the province would be met by force. Tensions eased as looted goods were returned.</p><p>From 1823 to 1828 Colombian troops under Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> occupied Upper Peru, as a continuation of the Campaigns of the South, and the new republic named after Bolívar was considered a satellite state of Gran Colombia. Argentina and Peru claimed parts of its territory on historical grounds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Peru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its armies and generals freed Venezuela, New Granada and Quito and also Peru and <b>Upper Peru</b>, which became Bolivia, and the union gave Bolívar the base for his wider plans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bolivia was created from <b>Upper Peru</b>, the territory of the Royal Audiencia of Charcas, whose silver mines at Potosí had made it one of the richest parts of the Spanish empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The silver mines of Potosí in <b>Upper Peru</b> (modern Bolivia), worked largely by indigenous labourers conscripted under the mita draft, and those of Zacatecas in Mexico, worked mainly by indigenous wage labour, made Spanish America the source of perhaps four-fifths of the world's silver between 1500 and 1800.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…former viceroy Santiago de Liniers was crushed and he was shot, but Paraguay and Montevideo swore to the Regency and the Viceroyalty of Peru annexed <b>Upper Peru</b>, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before any independence was declared.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the wars of independence Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal turned Peru into the royalist base of South America, sending armies that crushed revolutionaries in <b>Upper Peru</b> and Quito and reconquered Chile after the Battle of Rancagua in 1814.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The text was fiercely pro-independence and anti-Spanish, and its outlook reached beyond Argentina to the struggles in Mexico, Central America, northern South America and <b>Charcas</b>, while its allegory cast Spain itself, as well as Spanish absolutism, as the enemy.</blockquote><a href="/songs/argentine-national-anthem">Argentine National Anthem</a></li></ul></section>
